.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt PROCCTL 2
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm procctl
.Nd control processes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/procctl.h
.Ft int
.Fn procctl "idtype_t idtype" "id_t id" "int cmd" "void *data"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn procctl
applies the command
.Fa cmd
to the processes
.Fa idtype
and
.Fa id
name:
.Bl -tag -width P_PGID
.It Dv P_PID
the process
.Fa id ,
or the caller if
.Fa id
is 0;
.It Dv P_PGID
every process in process group
.Fa id ,
or in the caller's if
.Fa id
is 0.
.El
.Pp
A command applies to the whole of each process, all of its threads.
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width PROC_OOM_ADJ_STATUS
.It Dv PROC_OOM_ADJ_CTL
sets the process's OOM adjustment to the
.Vt int
.Fa data
points to, from
.Dv OOM_ADJ_MIN
(\-1000) to
.Dv OOM_ADJ_MAX
(1000).
When the system runs out of memory it kills the process with the most
private pages, each process's count weighted by
(1000 + adjustment) / 1000; a process whose adjustment is \-1000 is never
chosen, and neither is
.Xr init 8 .
A new process takes its parent's adjustment, and keeps it across
.Xr execve 2 .
Raising a process's adjustment needs the permission
.Xr kill 2
needs to signal it; lowering it needs the superuser.
Over a process group, the call succeeds if it could change any process.
.It Dv PROC_OOM_ADJ_STATUS
writes the process's OOM adjustment to the
.Vt int
.Fa data
points to.
.Dv P_PID
only.
.El
.Pp
The adjustment is also shown in
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /oom_adj .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std procctl
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa idtype
or
.Fa cmd
is not one of the above, the adjustment is out of range, or
.Dv PROC_OOM_ADJ_STATUS
was given a process group.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No process matches
.Fa idtype
and
.Fa id .
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not change any of the processes' adjustments.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa data
points outside the caller's memory.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr protect 1 ,
.Xr fork 2
.Sh STANDARDS
.Fn procctl
is not in POSIX.
.Sh HISTORY
FreeBSD 9.3 introduced
.Fn procctl .
OxideBSD's takes FreeBSD's form with its own commands and their own numbers;
it first appeared in OxideBSD 0.3.0.
